pipebuffer: don't hold the slab manager's mutex across a provider allocation

Against mesa-26.2.0 (9f0a761020bca92f2b07156a0621e5360cb8eca5), the commit
payload.spec.json pins. Not submitted upstream.

A GNOME session on the d3d12 driver freezes: gnome-shell's main thread stops on
a pthread mutex whose owner field holds its own thread id, and the desktop never
repaints again. The mouse pointer keeps moving, because mutter draws it from its
KMS thread, which makes the failure look like a stall in the present path. It is
not; it is a plain self-deadlock, and the whole of it is visible in one stack:

    _cogl_texture_set_region -> glTexSubImage2D -> st_TexSubImage
      d3d12_transfer_map                          needs a staging buffer
        pb_slab_manager_create_buffer             mtx_lock(&mgr->mutex)
          pb_slab_create                          no partial slab is left
            pb_cache_manager_create_buffer
              d3d12_bufmgr_create_buffer
                d3d12_bo_new
                  d3d12_screen_reclaim_completed  retires deferred frees
                    d3d12_bo_destroy
                      pb_reference(&bo->buffer, NULL)
                        pb_slab_buffer_destroy    mtx_lock(&mgr->mutex)

pb_slab_manager_create_buffer takes mgr->mutex and calls pb_slab_create under
it, which asks the provider below for the slab's backing buffer. For d3d12 that
provider is a pb_cache manager over d3d12_bufmgr, and d3d12_bo_new reclaims its
completed deferred frees before allocating -- so a buffer belonging to this same
slab manager can be released from inside the call, and pb_slab_buffer_destroy
reaches for a mutex the same thread is already holding. mgr->mutex is not
recursive, so the thread waits on itself.

It takes a coincidence to hit: the slab manager must be out of partial slabs at
the moment a deferred free has retired. That is why a desktop runs for a while,
through window switches and menus, and then stops.

The fix is to drop the mutex around the provider call. This is not a new idea in
Mesa: the newer pb_slab_alloc_reclaimed in util/pb_slab.c does exactly this, for
exactly this reason, and says so in a comment. The older manager in
pipebuffer/pb_bufmgr_slab.c, which is the one d3d12 uses, never got the same
treatment. Racing threads may then create more than one slab; the caller
re-reads mgr->slabs after pb_slab_create returns, and an untouched slab is only
idle memory, so correctness is unaffected -- again the same trade util/pb_slab.c
already accepts.

Refs: VMLord tasks #180, #188, #189, #190 for the d3d12 scanout path this
frees up.

--- a/src/gallium/auxiliary/pipebuffer/pb_bufmgr_slab.c	2026-09-05 16:11:17.510126091 +0400
+++ b/src/gallium/auxiliary/pipebuffer/pb_bufmgr_slab.c	2026-09-05 16:11:17.518126088 +0400
@@ -289,6 +289,9 @@
  * Create a new slab.
  * 
  * Called when we ran out of free slabs.
+ *
+ * Called with mgr->mutex held, and returns with it held -- but it does not hold
+ * it the whole way through; see the provider call below.
  */
 static enum pipe_error
 pb_slab_create(struct pb_slab_manager *mgr)
@@ -303,7 +306,23 @@
    if (!slab)
       return PIPE_ERROR_OUT_OF_MEMORY;
 
+   /* Drop the mutex temporarily to prevent a deadlock where the allocation
+    * calls back into slab functions. A provider is free to release buffers of
+    * its own while serving an allocation, and any of those may be a slab
+    * buffer of this very manager, whose pb_slab_buffer_destroy takes this
+    * mutex -- which is not recursive. The d3d12 driver does exactly that:
+    * d3d12_bo_new reclaims its completed deferred frees before it allocates.
+    *
+    * There's a chance that racing threads will end up allocating multiple
+    * slabs, but that doesn't hurt correctness: our caller re-reads mgr->slabs
+    * after we return, and a slab nobody took buffers from is merely idle.
+    *
+    * This is the same fix, for the same reason, that pb_slab_alloc_reclaimed
+    * in util/pb_slab.c already carries; this older manager never got it.
+    */
+   mtx_unlock(&mgr->mutex);
    slab->bo = mgr->provider->create_buffer(mgr->provider, mgr->slabSize, &mgr->desc);
+   mtx_lock(&mgr->mutex);
    if(!slab->bo) {
       ret = PIPE_ERROR_OUT_OF_MEMORY;
       goto out_err0;
